WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.245
My name is Benedikt Bunz,

00:00:01.245 --> 00:00:03.900
um, Ph.D. student here at Stanford,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.180
and I'm going to talk about Bulletproofs,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.800
which are short proofs for confidential transaction and a lot more, actually.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.675
And this is joint work with

00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:15.600
Jonathan Bootle from the University College of London, My adviser,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.990
Van Bunday, and Andrew, Peter and Greg,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.635
or Andrew and Peter from Blockstream, and Greg Maxwell.

00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:26.700
So- and this is a talk about, uh,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.115
so if you've- you might have seen as this is

00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:32.850
kind of a depic- depiction of what a Bitcoin transaction,

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.660
uh, looks like, but the same thing applies to other cryptocurrencies.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.330
Very similarly, and, and the important thing to

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.940
note is that there's some sort of inputs on

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.505
the left that come into the transaction and some sort of

00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:47.370
outputs that go out of the transaction.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.145
And it is important that the inputs

00:00:50.145 --> 00:00:54.975
are greater or x equal to the output or the outputs are less than,

00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:58.860
uh, the inputs and actually the difference between the outputs and inputs is

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.455
exactly the fee that is sort of paid to the miners for verifying the transactions.

00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:06.570
This is all you need to understand about Bitcoin and Blockchain.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:11.015
You don't, um, thi- this is just some generic transaction format.

00:01:11.015 --> 00:01:15.000
And so, when is a Bitcoin transaction valid?

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.170
Well there's three criteria and really,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.555
uh, one of them is that the signature is correct.

00:01:19.555 --> 00:01:20.985
So, that there is some person,

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:22.170
you know, who owns the,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.590
uh, inputs authorizes that the transaction is being spent.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.290
The second one is that the inputs are unspent.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.205
So, you cannot spend money twice.

00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:34.320
And then the third one is the one that we're going to care most about today which

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.220
is that the sum of the inputs is equal to the sum of the outputs plus the fees.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.675
So, one problem with, uh,

00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:45.500
Bitcoin transactions and many other cryptocurrency transactions

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.685
is that they're not confidential.

00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:50.200
They nee- they don't have any privacy, so they are neither, uh,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.115
what we call confidential or, uh, anonymous.

00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:55.985
And by confidentiality, I mean that

00:01:55.985 --> 00:02:01.025
the amounts are hidden and by anonymity I mean that you can see who is paying whom.

00:02:01.025 --> 00:02:04.910
Um, and you can see these addresses, uh, you know they,

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:10.820
they hide like it doesn't say that who's paying whom in terms of real world identities,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.960
but it actually turns out that it's quite easy to figure out who owns which address,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.510
and then you can clearly see how much money is being

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.330
transferred it's publicly available on the Blockchain,

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.385
and you can see who is paying whom.

00:02:22.385 --> 00:02:24.085
So, why is this a problem?

00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:28.180
Well, um, this has many apli- implications for businesses.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.120
Especially the transaction amounts being available in the clear.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.860
And, and, for example,

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.320
it means that if you receive your salary in,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.130
in Bitcoin or some other similar cryptocurrency it is that- uh,

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.450
your salary will be public,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.580
and everybody can just look up what your salary is.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.090
And also if you're a business and you're buying some sort of tools from

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.950
your suppliers say you know Ford buys tires from, from, uh, Michelin,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.740
whatever, and then, uh, they can see,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.525
everybody can publicly see how much is being paid for these goods,

00:02:58.525 --> 00:03:01.260
and these are important business secrets that business would want to hide.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.420
So, this really makes, um, uh,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.180
blockchains in some ways at least, uh,

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.480
less usable for, for businesses.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.785
So, to combat this is- there's this idea about of,

00:03:11.785 --> 00:03:16.710
of confidential transactions which Greg Maxwell came up in 2016.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:20.990
And the idea is that instead of sending the amounts in the clear,

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.440
we, we encrypt them or more, ah,

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.320
correctly we use the cryptographic commitment

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.080
which is kind of like saying we put the amounts in an envelope.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.350
A cryptographic commitment is something that does not reveal the value,

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:38.755
but after the fact I can only open it to be the value that I originally committed to.

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:40.660
So, just like an envelope.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.390
And then as concretely we would use

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.260
these Pedersen commitments which are discrete log based.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.080
So, the problem now obviously if no one or

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.529
if it's publicly not visible what the amounts are,

00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:58.220
how can we check that the transaction is valid and especially how can we check

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.375
that third constraint that the sum of inputs is greater or equal to the sum of outputs.

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:07.490
And it actually turns out that there's a second thing that we need to check even if,

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.305
if we have this you know this equality check or

00:04:10.305 --> 00:04:13.370
there's this greater equal check from the inputs and outputs.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:17.585
And it turns out that we also need to check that both of the outputs are positive.

00:04:17.585 --> 00:04:21.285
Right? Because otherwise if I have five Bitcoin going in,

00:04:21.285 --> 00:04:24.520
and I have minus two and seven Bitcoin going out,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.700
then the first check goes through,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.630
but the second check obviously doesn't go through,

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.080
and I've now sa- uh, created seven,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.600
I've a seven-Bitcoin output which is a big problem.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.775
And the second, uh, test, the,

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.090
the positive outputs is actually

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:41.360
the more difficult one and that the one that we'll focus on today.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.790
The other one is, is simpler because there's

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:47.760
this additive homomorphic property of the commitment scheme.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.805
Um, so, these confidential transactions were proposed in 2016 as I said,

00:04:53.805 --> 00:04:55.110
and it is, uh,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.265
kind of the structure is similar to a Bitcoin transaction.

00:04:58.265 --> 00:05:02.130
The transaction graph, so who's paying whom is still publicly available.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.750
So, we do not hide that there's

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.550
other cryptocurrencies like Zcash or Monero that that use,

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:11.810
uh, that have tools kind of for hiding the transaction graph.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:16.485
But, the question is how do we achieve this public verifiability of transaction validity.

00:05:16.485 --> 00:05:18.930
Well, luckily there's this, uh,

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.510
tool called zero knowledge proof of knowledge.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.380
So, in a zero knowledge proof of knowledge,

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.295
you have a prover and a verifier.

00:05:25.295 --> 00:05:28.680
And the prover, Peggy wants to convince the verifier Victor

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.170
that this commitment that she has is actually a commitment to a positive number.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.690
And, uh, traditionally this works through some sort of

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:41.160
challenge response protocol where Victor gets to ask questions to Peggy,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.290
and Peggy can only answer

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:47.280
these questions if the statement that she's trying to prove is true.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.415
If not, then she will be unable,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.950
or with high probability, she will be unable to answer these questions.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.930
But, the answers to the questions,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.915
her responses do not reveal any information about the,

00:05:58.915 --> 00:06:03.100
the, the kind of secret information that she's using to prove the statements.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.330
[NOISE] So, it doesn't reveal the X or the R in this case.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.700
And after this protocol is run,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.280
and this can be a multiple round protocol, um,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.920
Victor will be convinced that Peggy knows this value X and

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.125
that X is positive but he has still no idea what exactly the value X is.

00:06:22.125 --> 00:06:24.685
And this is called, uh,

00:06:24.685 --> 00:06:27.260
concretely what we're trying to prove here would be called Range Proof.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:32.410
So, you can prove that X is a number between say zero and two to the 52.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.320
And it turns out that we can make this whole thing

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.320
even non-interactive and publicly verifiable.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.610
So, this is kind of, common reference string and,

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.205
and Peggy can just write down a proof and then after the fact

00:06:45.205 --> 00:06:49.580
Victor or someone else can, can check the proof and put it,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.095
uh, and, and be convinced that X is in fact positive.

00:06:53.095 --> 00:06:57.130
And so for example this is a proof that you could put on the Blockchain attached to

00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:02.125
your transaction and everybody is then convinced that your transaction is in fact valid.

00:07:02.125 --> 00:07:04.530
So, how could we make such a proof?

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.760
Well, at a very high level,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.940
what does it mean for a number to be in some range,

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.330
so between zero and two to the say 64 bits.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:18.100
Well, what it means is that I can write it down using 64 bits.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:22.220
Right? That's, uh, a number between zero and two to the 64.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.035
I can write down using 64 bits.

00:07:24.035 --> 00:07:27.070
So, one idea is that Peggy will create

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:31.855
a commitment for each of the bits and then send over these,

00:07:31.855 --> 00:07:34.415
you know n commitments.

00:07:34.415 --> 00:07:36.755
If we're trying to prove to be that,

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:40.635
that X is between zero and two to the 64 minus one actually.

00:07:40.635 --> 00:07:42.700
Um, and a proof,

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:46.240
and the proof says that each of the committed values, these little, uh,

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.750
committed values is in fact a commitment to either zero or one and that the, uh,

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.820
the big commitment to the whole number is,

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.535
uh, the, the, the combination of these big commitments.

00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:01.085
So, the problem with this, uh, uh,

00:08:01.085 --> 00:08:05.410
range proof and this is what we could call a linear range proof and you can base it

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:10.115
on the Sigma protocols and use the Fiat-Shamir heuristic to make it non-interactive,

00:08:10.115 --> 00:08:11.790
is that they're fairly large.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:17.605
So, they're four kilobytes even with some recent optimizations for a 64-bit range proof.

00:08:17.605 --> 00:08:19.645
And if I try to increase the bit,

00:08:19.645 --> 00:08:21.130
the number of bits in my range,

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.825
then the proof would grow linearly.

00:08:22.825 --> 00:08:27.980
Right? Because I had to send over even just one commitment per- um,

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.170
per- ah bit in my range.

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:36.305
So, ah, the nice thing is they don't have a trusted set and I'll talk about that in,

00:08:36.305 --> 00:08:37.820
in a second But,

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.580
uh, four kilobytes for, you know,

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.810
this would mean a four kilobyte proof being attached to every transaction that is

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:49.240
not really practical today with what blockchains can handle today.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.925
So, they are shorter proofs.

00:08:51.925 --> 00:08:53.405
Uh, and, and one of the,

00:08:53.405 --> 00:08:56.135
the and they're called these preprocessing

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:59.990
SNARKs and what they do is there's kind of a entrusted setup.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.570
So, in the beginning there's some sort of some party

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:05.930
that creates these queries, encrypts them,

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:11.490
sends them to the prover and gives the verifier some advice string that will help it to,

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.015
to check the answers to these queries.

00:09:14.015 --> 00:09:15.720
And you can actually create really,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.755
really short proofs and, and these proofs are,

00:09:19.755 --> 00:09:24.050
are you know a couple like 200 bytes irrespective of,

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.845
of the complexity of the statement that you're trying to prove.

00:09:26.845 --> 00:09:29.350
So, no matter whether it's a short range proof or

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:32.135
a long range proof or an even more complex statement,

00:09:32.135 --> 00:09:35.585
the size of the proof is only a couple hundred bytes.

00:09:35.585 --> 00:09:40.355
The problem with this is that and verification is also fast which is nice,

00:09:40.355 --> 00:09:43.790
the problem with this is that say the person who does the set

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:47.990
up is actually malicious and for

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:51.650
example colludes with the prover or tries to create a proof by

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:55.930
himself then he can suddenly- he can create fake proofs.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:59.420
So, he convin- can convince you of statements that aren't true.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:02.570
So, you could commit to a negative number and prove to you

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:05.810
that it is a positive number and the verifier would be fooled.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:09.755
Um, so, uh-

00:10:09.755 --> 00:10:13.700
Uh, why is this, um,

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.790
why is this a problem, why are these trusted setups

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:18.185
a problem especially in, in cryptocurrencies?

00:10:18.185 --> 00:10:20.195
Well, the problem is that,

00:10:20.195 --> 00:10:22.470
what does it mean to create a fake proof?

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:27.060
Well, if the subverted prover of the setup is subverted, then,

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.680
uh, this is A,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.880
this is undetectable, there's no way to really test for it.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.835
And B, then you can create these transactions which have,

00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:37.870
say negative amounts of- in them,

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.755
and what that really means is that I can create money out of thin air.

00:10:40.755 --> 00:10:44.395
It is inflation and it is even undetectable inflation,

00:10:44.395 --> 00:10:46.500
because now all the amounts are being hidden, right?

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.160
It's not like, in, in Bitcoin where all the amounts are public

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.030
and you can check how much total money there is.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:54.490
If, uh, the trusted setup is being subverted

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:58.500
then no one knows how much money there is and,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.135
um, there's really, you know,

00:11:00.135 --> 00:11:05.010
it's- it's undetectable inflation and an attacker could really extract a lot of money,

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.790
uh, from the system, so it's a,

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:08.970
it's a very bad situation.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.745
And the problem is that even if you don't have a subverted setup,

00:11:13.745 --> 00:11:16.050
even the fear of this, uh,

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.210
undetectable inflation is dangerous.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.735
So for example, Zcash used this,

00:11:20.735 --> 00:11:23.840
uses these SNARKs and there's people going around saying or,

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.315
you know, that are saying, you know,

00:11:26.315 --> 00:11:28.075
it's not secure this subverted- uh,

00:11:28.075 --> 00:11:30.135
the set up is subverted, which,

00:11:30.135 --> 00:11:31.705
you know they did a very good job at,

00:11:31.705 --> 00:11:35.420
at trying to prevent that but there's really nothing more that you can say.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:37.490
There's no proof that you can, uh,

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.615
give to people to say,

00:11:38.615 --> 00:11:41.485
no, the setup was done correctly.

00:11:41.485 --> 00:11:44.260
Um, and this fear can lower for example

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:46.670
the price of the cryptocurrency and the value of it and,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:48.970
and the will- willingness of people to use it.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:52.870
So, the way to alleviate this is usually through this kind of

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:56.960
distributed setup and so-called multiparty computation.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:02.290
And this is difficult but Zcash actually did that an- and- and is doing that,

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.900
um, for, er, but it's, you know,

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:09.280
this- you can read up on it and the Zcash ceremony there is a great podcast on it,

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.710
I think by, uh,

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:12.320
Planet Money or something and it's,

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.010
uh, yeah, they had to do a lot of work to do this and it's kind of annoying

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:20.235
and you still don't solve all the problems.

00:12:20.235 --> 00:12:22.140
And also every time you add

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:26.100
some new functionality it means that you have a new setup, right?

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.960
Every time you change even the slightest thing,

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.480
you would have to redo the setup.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:31.940
So they have to actually redo the setup right now.

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:33.710
So that again is a problem.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:35.110
Er, so for example,

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:36.450
there's Hawk which is, you know,

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:40.150
smart- is kind of a smart contracts, uh,

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:43.000
confidential smart contract or private smart contract,

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.550
a proposal for that, a paper.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.780
And what they had is that there's a different trusted setup

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:51.595
for every single smart contract and that clearly doesn't seem feasible.

00:12:51.595 --> 00:12:53.820
So, with that in mind,

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.000
we were looking for, you know,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.010
other proof systems that could help,

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:59.720
uh, that did not have this trusted setup,

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.835
uh, but still could be used for these range proofs.

00:13:02.835 --> 00:13:07.190
So there is this paper by- by Jonathan Bootle and,

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:08.925
and the group at, uh,

00:13:08.925 --> 00:13:13.860
University College London, which are these log-sized proofs for arithmetic circuit.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:18.020
So, um, this is a proof protocol again for

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:22.480
arithmetic circuit which means for kind of arbitrary computation,

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.960
and the nice thing is that the proofs are

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.640
only logarithmic in the size of the computation.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.925
So asymptotically, they seem great and it only relies,

00:13:30.925 --> 00:13:35.190
it also doesn't have a trusted setup and only relies on the discrete log assumption.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.655
The problem was that in practice,

00:13:37.655 --> 00:13:40.050
if you actually wanted to do a range proof,

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:43.355
you would have to do a proof that, you know,

00:13:43.355 --> 00:13:47.790
this value you would have to implement the commitment function which is

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:50.510
a multi-exponentiation within the circuit

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:53.170
and things get rather expensive and in practice,

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.760
they would not have been much better than- than the linear protocol that

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.530
was out there especially in size and probably a lot worse in terms of,

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.540
uh, proving and verification time.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:05.575
But, um, we took, uh,

00:14:05.575 --> 00:14:09.940
we- we looked at their protocol and changed it and improved it such

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:14.195
that it would work better for these range proofs and also works better in general.

00:14:14.195 --> 00:14:17.375
And this is how we came up with full proofs,

00:14:17.375 --> 00:14:21.175
um, which allows you now to do proofs on committed values.

00:14:21.175 --> 00:14:23.025
And what that means is that,

00:14:23.025 --> 00:14:27.800
I can  do a proof on something that is within the commitment.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.750
I don't have to implement the commitment function or

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:34.670
the opening function of the commitment within the arithmetic circuit.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:38.215
So, and also we reduce the proof size by,

00:14:38.215 --> 00:14:40.700
by a factor of three, um,

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:45.795
which means that now you'll only have to give two log two of n elements

00:14:45.795 --> 00:14:48.755
for a range proof and

00:14:48.755 --> 00:14:52.735
these improvements also transfer over to arbitrary arithmetic circuits,

00:14:52.735 --> 00:14:55.640
which means, when I say arithmetic circuits, I mean that,

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:00.720
I can prove to you that I know some secret inputs to a circuit or a,

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.695
or a function, um,

00:15:02.695 --> 00:15:05.510
such that the function evaluates to say one.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.995
Okay. And I don't have to give you, uh,

00:15:07.995 --> 00:15:10.000
what these values are but I can convince you that I

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.350
know these secret values so you can, uh,

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:17.210
implement more complex, uh, functionalities with this.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:20.995
So, um, let's see what this, uh,

00:15:20.995 --> 00:15:22.920
kind of this improvement to, like,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.400
two log n elements,

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.415
what does this mean in practice?

00:15:26.415 --> 00:15:29.345
Well, uh, for a single range proof,

00:15:29.345 --> 00:15:31.760
this means that, um,

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.710
our proofs are 670 bytes versus

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:39.705
the previous range proof which was four kilobytes and SNARKs are,

00:15:39.705 --> 00:15:42.230
you know, 188 bytes.

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:45.330
Uh, so they clearly knocked the ball out of the park but,

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:46.695
er, they are, uh,

00:15:46.695 --> 00:15:48.535
they have the problem with the trusted setup.

00:15:48.535 --> 00:15:50.510
The nice thing is that, you know,

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:54.980
a transaction as I depic- depicted earlier usually has multiple outputs.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:57.620
So, say it has two outputs and

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.320
I want to prove to you that both of these outputs are positive,

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.520
which is the usual situation.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:06.610
Well, it turns out because we have this logarithmic scaling, this,

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:11.070
uh, we can just create one bulletproof for both of these outputs.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:14.750
Um, and what that means is that suddenly now, uh,

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:19.605
both of these proofs together are just 736 bytes, so,

00:16:19.605 --> 00:16:22.340
we just added 64 bytes to,

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:25.345
um, double the number of proofs that we're doing.

00:16:25.345 --> 00:16:27.590
Whereas the previous proof would scale linearly,

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:29.240
so it would be eight kilobytes.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:31.100
And this goes on, you know,

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:32.895
if I have 10 proofs it's, uh,

00:16:32.895 --> 00:16:37.435
928 bytes versus, uh, almost 40,000 bytes.

00:16:37.435 --> 00:16:42.240
So, the more outputs the- the larger the statement is that you're trying to prove,

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.290
the larger the savings are, um,

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:50.340
that you're, uh, that- that bulletproofs has versus these traditional proofs.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.020
So, when would, uh,

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:54.455
we ask ourselves well when would,

00:16:54.455 --> 00:16:57.410
would these transactions ever have that many outputs, right?

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:01.505
Like, you know, you might send some Bitcoin to your whole family for Christmas but,

00:17:01.505 --> 00:17:04.295
uh, usually you're right like yo- you only have two outputs.

00:17:04.295 --> 00:17:09.870
So, uh, but there's multiple people at the same time that are trying to do,

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:12.610
uh, a transaction and trying to create a bulletproof.

00:17:12.610 --> 00:17:16.165
So, we looked at this situation where there's multiple provers,

00:17:16.165 --> 00:17:17.770
each of them have a commitment, right?

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.580
Each of them want to send some money, um,

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:27.660
and they want to create a single proof without revealing the secrets to each other, um,

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.610
that all of the- their commitments commit to say

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:34.455
positive numbers or they want to prove something about these commitments but,

00:17:34.455 --> 00:17:37.115
uh, concretely we cared about range proofs.

00:17:37.115 --> 00:17:40.170
And, uh, well how can they create one proof?

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:45.365
Well, trivial solutions, obviously they can concatenate their proofs but,

00:17:45.365 --> 00:17:48.925
um, we would want something that is,

00:17:48.925 --> 00:17:51.580
uh, something that is, er, something where the proof is small.

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:58.995
So, um, the generic way to do this is- is a multi-party computation, um,

00:17:58.995 --> 00:18:05.065
but it turns out that we can create or the- the prover is extremely structured,

00:18:05.065 --> 00:18:09.270
so we can create this custom multi-party computation which allows

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:14.750
these provers to kind of create one proof together instead of having multiple proofs.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:19.330
And, uh, this works whenever kind of each of them wants

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:24.155
to prove a separate statement and then the- the overall sta- statement is just,

00:18:24.155 --> 00:18:26.820
uh, a conjunction of all of their statements.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:30.054
So it's, you know, prover one wants to say, uh,

00:18:30.054 --> 00:18:33.550
my value is in some range and then prover two wants to say

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.300
my value is in another range and prover

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:37.810
three wants to prove something completely different,

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:41.430
but, uh, it's just an and between these three things.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:45.035
So, um, the nice thing is that,

00:18:45.035 --> 00:18:49.110
uh, we can do this in a logarithmic number of rounds,

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.690
so there has to be some communication and with

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:55.345
the logarithmic amount of- of computa- communication,

00:18:55.345 --> 00:18:57.160
or there's a different protocol,

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:58.640
uh, which just has, uh,

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:02.675
three rounds but has linear communications or more communication.

00:19:02.675 --> 00:19:06.100
So there's a tradeoff kind of that we provide and there's,

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.470
you know, multiple levels in between that.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:16.100
Um, so, the nice thing is that this allows us- there's already this protocol for,

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:18.555
um, which is called,

00:19:18.555 --> 00:19:23.990
uh, I think I have it on the next slide what it's called but there's this protocol for

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.470
combining transactions where multiple users can

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.670
combine transactions and the traditional reason why this was,

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:35.060
ah, used is because people wanted to hide who they're sending money to, right?

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:38.110
So you get some added anonymity from that,

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.880
but now with confidential transactions and with

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:44.840
bulletproofs it actually makes the average size,

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:46.400
or it makes the size of the proof.

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:48.990
And by that, the transaction fees, uh,

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:52.680
which scale linearly in the size of the transaction, it makes it cheaper.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:55.160
So there's even economic incentives to combine

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:58.780
your transactions into one transaction that is smaller.

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:01.600
So, uh, what are the kind of,

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.340
you know, what are the results of this?

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:06.480
How can- can bulletproof help for confidential transaction or

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:10.655
this- this is also this new Blockchain format called Mimblewimble.

00:20:10.655 --> 00:20:13.740
Well, as I said it's 670 bytes instead of

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:17.675
four kilobytes per range proof and a 64 bit range,

00:20:17.675 --> 00:20:20.645
but again then this aggregation really,

00:20:20.645 --> 00:20:24.005
uh, helps to make this gap a lot bigger.

00:20:24.005 --> 00:20:25.790
So for 16 range proofs,

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:28.770
it's 930 bytes versus 61 kilobytes.

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:33.010
And doubling at the- doubling the precision would only add 64 bytes, right?

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:34.640
Again this is just,

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:36.590
uh, this logarithmic scaling.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:42.549
Uh, and concretely if- if kind of the structure of transaction didn't change,

00:20:42.549 --> 00:20:46.175
uh, which we would assume it would because there's new incentives,

00:20:46.175 --> 00:20:51.510
even then, uh, if you just applied the old confidential transactions to Bitcoin,

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:58.605
the UTXO set would be 160 gigabytes and now with bulletproofs it would be 17 gigabytes.

00:20:58.605 --> 00:21:01.590
And this is the protocol that I was talking about was called

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:05.100
CoinJoin [LAUGHTER] that was the name that I can think of.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:10.340
So, its has this kind of built-in simple CoinJoin protocol for combining,

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:13.325
uh, transactions into a single one.

00:21:13.325 --> 00:21:16.400
Um, we also looked at whether- so the, the,

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.005
the protocol is susceptible to- to kind of

00:21:19.005 --> 00:21:22.530
quantum attacks and- and we looked at things that,

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:27.605
um, versus, uh, it's susceptible to attacks by a quantum computer.

00:21:27.605 --> 00:21:29.665
Um, and we looked at things that,

00:21:29.665 --> 00:21:31.445
uh, perhaps, you know, uh,

00:21:31.445 --> 00:21:36.650
where a quantum computer could only break the hiding property, uh,

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:39.950
of the, the proofs so the zero knowledge property of the proof,

00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:43.095
but not the actual so-called soundness.

00:21:43.095 --> 00:21:45.480
Um, the problem is this, uh,

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:49.120
so the idea is that, um.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.580
An attacker could reveal or see how much money everybody is spending,

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:55.300
so break the privacy, but not,

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:56.945
uh, print new money.

00:21:56.945 --> 00:22:00.015
It turns out that because we're using compression,

00:22:00.015 --> 00:22:01.860
so it turns out because, uh,

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:05.745
we want our proofs to be actually smaller than the statement that we're proving,

00:22:05.745 --> 00:22:10.850
this is, uh, it's impossible to, to get this unconditional soundness.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.400
So as I said, uh, you know,

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.590
bulletproofs also works with these arbitrary statements.

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:22.870
Uh, so for example you could use it on Zcash and the proof size would

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:29.045
be 1.7 kilobytes but actually Zcash is improving kind of their circuit and that would help,

00:22:29.045 --> 00:22:32.800
uh, bulletproofs a lot so it would be 1.3 Kilobytes.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:38.080
There's a reason why Zcash can probably not switch to bulletproof which is, uh,

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:41.140
that the verification time the proof size scales

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:45.340
logarithmic and the size of the proof but the verification time is still linear.

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:49.320
So the more complex if I'm trying to prove to you that, uh,

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:51.170
statement that is twice as complex,

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:54.545
verifying it will take roughly twice as long.

00:22:54.545 --> 00:22:58.250
Um, so this is an overview over kind of

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:03.650
different proof systems and the short takeaway from this is that there's multiple,

00:23:03.650 --> 00:23:06.465
you know, this state- this space is really developing.

00:23:06.465 --> 00:23:08.805
There's multiple different proof systems out there,

00:23:08.805 --> 00:23:11.590
each of them has different benefits and it really

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:14.745
depends on the application which one is the best one to use.

00:23:14.745 --> 00:23:20.850
So, you know, SNARKs are extremely efficient in terms of verifier time and proof size,

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:23.840
but they have this trusted set up, um,

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:29.300
and you know the bulletproofs are short but the verifier takes linear time,

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:33.785
so this doesn't work for if you know the statement you're trying to prove is too complex.

00:23:33.785 --> 00:23:35.810
So, um, you know,

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:39.440
if if you have to really think about your application,

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:42.270
uh, when there's no clear winner out there.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:44.870
So you have to do some thinking, uh,

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:48.155
and it's really application specific of what the best tool is.

00:23:48.155 --> 00:23:52.270
Um, I just want to touch on like one, uh,

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:56.905
or maybe two other things where where bulletproofs can also help.

00:23:56.905 --> 00:23:59.880
So we had this protocol, um,

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:05.730
in that we developed in 2015 which allowed Bitcoin exchanges to prove

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:08.305
that they're solvent so that they have enough money

00:24:08.305 --> 00:24:11.760
for paying out all their customers without revealing any information.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.930
So without revealing how much money they have in total

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:16.460
or what their Bitcoin addresses are,

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.240
uh, or any of these information.

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.450
So it's kind of a zero knowledge proof for solvency.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:24.590
And with bulletproofs this would dramatically reduce

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:29.955
the size of the proof from like 18 gigabytes to a couple megabytes.

00:24:29.955 --> 00:24:33.040
Another application outside the realm of blockchains

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.895
is something called a verifiable shuffle.

00:24:35.895 --> 00:24:39.390
So this is used in so-called mix nets where, uh,

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:41.430
each sender, you know, wants to,

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:42.600
there's a couple of senders,

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:44.505
each of them has an email and there's uh,

00:24:44.505 --> 00:24:48.670
receivers and you want to somehow have this interactive protocol which hides

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:53.840
which message is going to whom and one core component of this is,

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:58.525
uh, you need to produce these zero knowledge proofs that the inputs and outputs are that,

00:24:58.525 --> 00:25:02.930
uh, you know unencrypted inputs and unencrypted outputs are the same.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:05.205
And it turns out that,

00:25:05.205 --> 00:25:06.980
uh, you know, with bulletproofs it's,

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:11.990
it's the first logarithmic size verifiable shuffle without a trusted setup.

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:13.780
So this is, this proving that the inputs and

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:16.050
outputs are the same is called a verifiable shuffle.

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:20.240
Right, I, I, um, shuffle the values and I can prove to you that I did it correctly,

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:25.175
um, and with bulletproofs we can do this very efficiently.

00:25:25.175 --> 00:25:30.345
Um, so we also implemented this or, or, uh,

00:25:30.345 --> 00:25:34.500
let's say Andrew and Peter did an amazing job at implementing this

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:38.505
into the Bitcoin cryptographic library with, uh,

00:25:38.505 --> 00:25:40.780
you know the prover's constant time so there's

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:43.330
no timing attacks there and the verify's

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:44.870
extremely fast and I know that there's

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:48.760
multiple other implementations and progress are already out there.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:51.760
And, uh, one of the main things that we did when,

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:54.370
when implementing is is kind of boiling down

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:57.475
this this very complex verification protocol,

00:25:57.475 --> 00:26:00.965
to this one simple big multi-exponentiation.

00:26:00.965 --> 00:26:02.230
Which is just as this,

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:04.325
this check that you see here on the right.

00:26:04.325 --> 00:26:07.700
So, if I want to check a proof I just kind of have to do this at base.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:12.280
It almost boils down I'm hiding some detail to this, uh, one check.

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:17.335
So the reason why this is interesting is because multi-exponentiation is,

00:26:17.335 --> 00:26:20.040
uh, to some degree it's, it's sublinear.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.650
So it scales with N over logN

00:26:22.650 --> 00:26:26.020
and this actually gives us some benefits and it turns out that for,

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:29.520
for small circuits verifying, uh,

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.445
so for example for range proofs,

00:26:31.445 --> 00:26:36.275
verifying a bulletproof is even faster than verifying a SNARK.

00:26:36.275 --> 00:26:39.480
The other thing that this multi-exponentiation gives us,

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:41.985
is that say I have two bulletproofs, okay.

00:26:41.985 --> 00:26:45.989
And then I have these two big multi-exponentiations

00:26:45.989 --> 00:26:49.825
that I need to verify with only the exponents are different.

00:26:49.825 --> 00:26:52.690
Most of the generators kind of the things at

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:55.915
the bottom and are the same but the exponents are different.

00:26:55.915 --> 00:26:57.460
Well, it turns out that there's

00:26:57.460 --> 00:27:03.704
this batch verification technique that was originally designed for signatures, um,

00:27:03.704 --> 00:27:06.630
but it also applies here, where you can kind of,

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:09.560
you just combine these equations by raising

00:27:09.560 --> 00:27:13.745
the first one to a random scalar alpha and then adding them together.

00:27:13.745 --> 00:27:15.600
And what this means is that,

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.690
I need to do a couple more scalar operations,

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:23.605
but I still only need to do the same number of, uh exponentiations.

00:27:23.605 --> 00:27:27.080
And, uh, if you've ever implemented cryptography,

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:28.190
it turns out that these,

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:32.105
these exponentiations are a lot more expensive than the scalar operations.

00:27:32.105 --> 00:27:37.440
So in effect what this does is that verifying the first proof is this

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:43.065
I think for a 64 bit range proof is 6.2 milliseconds and,

00:27:43.065 --> 00:27:47.920
uh, and verifying the second one is only 550 microseconds.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:50.530
So, a factor of 10 times less.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:52.630
And you know if you're for example a miner

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:55.195
and want to verify a whole block of transactions,

00:27:55.195 --> 00:28:00.180
uh, your amortized cost is really closer to the 550 microseconds.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.610
So, um, yeah, thank you very much.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:09.455
The the web site for where you can find the paper and links to the implementations,

00:28:09.455 --> 00:28:12.585
um, is uh, crypto.stanford.edu/bulletproof.

00:28:12.585 --> 00:28:19.030
Thank you very much [APPLAUSE].

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:24.030
Any questions?

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:27.360
Can you say a bit about where where it's going to be used.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:31.690
Yeah. So, um, Monero which is I think the 10th largest

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:36.260
cryptocurrency actually they announced that they will implement this,

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:38.390
uh, because they have, uh,

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.650
you know they've been struggling with their transaction size

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.405
because they use the traditional confidential transactions.

00:28:43.405 --> 00:28:47.590
And, um, so they will actually implement this and I think,

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:50.810
you know, their goal is by the end of the year, uh,

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:57.134
that bullet proofs will secure this multibillion dollar, uh, cryptocurrency,

00:28:57.134 --> 00:28:58.660
which is you know it's, it's,

00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:00.215
uh, in some ways it's, uh,

